24. The general definition of Napierian logarithms is that, if <math>e^{x+\iota y} = a+\iota b</math>, then <math>x+\iota y = \log\ (a+\iota b)</math>. Now we know that '''''Theory of Logarithms.''''' <math>e^{x+\iota y} = e^x \cos\ y + \iota e^x\ \sin\ y</math>; hence <math>e^x\ \cos\ y = a</math>, <math>e^x\ \sin\ y = b</math>, or <math>e^x = (a^2+b^2)^{\frac{1}{2}}</math>, <math>y = \text{arc}\ \tan\ b/a \pm m\pi</math>, where <math>m</math> is an integer. If <math>b = 0</math>, then <math>m</math> must be even or odd according as <math>a</math> is positive or negative; hence
{{block center|<math>\log_e\ (a+\iota b) = \log_e\ (a^2+b^2)^{\frac{1}{2}} + \iota\ (\text{arc}\ \tan\ b/a \pm 2n\pi)</math>}}
{{block center|or <math>\log_e\ (a+\iota b) = \log_e\ (a^2+b^2)^{\frac{1}{2}} + \iota\ (\text{arc}\ \tan\ b/a \pm \overline{2n+\pi}),</math>}}
according as <math>a</math> is positive or negative. Thus the logarithm of any complex or real quantity is a multiple-valued function, the difference between successive values being <math>2\pi\iota</math>; in particular, '''''Hyperbolic Trigonometry.''''' the most general form of the logarithm of a real positive quantity is obtained by adding positive or negative multiples of <math>2\pi\iota</math> to the arithmetical logarithm. On this subject, see De Morgan's ''Trigonometry and Double Algebra'', ch. iv., and a paper by Professor Cayley in vol. ii. of ''Proc. London Math. Soc.''

25. We have from the definitions given in § 21, <math>\cos\ \iota y = \tfrac{1}{2}(e^y+e^{-y})</math> and <math>\sin\ \iota y = \tfrac{1}{2}i(e^y-e^{-y})</math>. The expressions, <math>\tfrac{1}{2}(e^y+e^{-y})</math>, <math>\tfrac{1}{2}(e^y-e^{-y})</math> are said to define the hyperbolic cosine and sine of <math>y</math> and are written <math>\cosh\ y</math>, <math>\sinh\ y</math>; thus <math>\cosh\ y = \cos\ \iota y</math>, <math>\sinh\ y = -\iota\ \sin\ \iota y</math>. The functions <math>\cosh\ y</math>, <math>\sinh\ y</math> are connected with the rectangular hyperbola in a manner analogous to that in which the cosine and

readings.c2s1:
sine are connected with the circle. We may easily show from the definitions that

{{block center|<math>\cos^2(x+\iota y)+\sin^2(x+\iota y)=1,</math>}}

{{block center|<math>\cosh^2 y-\sinh^2 y=1;</math>}}

{{block center|<math>\cos(x+\iota y)=\cos x\cosh y-\iota\sin x\sinh y,</math>}}

{{block center|<math>\sin(x+\iota y)=\sin x\cosh y+\iota\cos x\sinh y,</math>}}

{{block center|<math>\cosh(\alpha+\beta)=\cosh\alpha\cosh\beta+\sinh\alpha\sinh\beta,</math>}}

{{block center|<math>\sinh(\alpha+\beta)=\sinh\alpha\cosh\beta+\cosh\alpha\sinh\beta.</math>}}

These formulae are the basis of a complete hyperbolic trigonometry. The connexion of these functions with the hyperbola was first pointed out by Lambert.

