26. If we equate the coefficients of <math>n</math> on both sides of equation (13), this process requiring, however, a justification of its validity, we get

{{block center|<math>\theta=\sin\theta+\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\sin^3\theta}{3}+\frac{1.3}{2.4}\,\frac{\sin^5\theta}{5}+\frac{1.3\cdot5}{2.4.6}\,\frac{\sin^7\theta}{7}+\dots;</math> (21)}}

<math>\theta</math> must lie between the values <math>\pm\tfrac{1}{2}\pi</math>. This equation may also be written in the form

{{block center|<math>\text{arc}\sin x=x+\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{x^3}{3}+\frac{1.3}{2.4}\,\frac{x^5}{5}+\frac{1.3\cdot5}{2.4.6}\,\frac{x^7}{7}+\dots</math>}}

when <math>x</math> lies between <math>\pm1</math>.

By equating the coefficients of <math>n^2</math> on both sides of equation (12) we get

{{block center|<math>\theta^2=\sin^2\theta+\frac{2}{3}\,\frac{\sin^4\theta}{2}+\frac{2.4}{3\cdot5}\,\frac{\sin^6\theta}{3}+\frac{2.4.6}{3\cdot5\cdot7}\,\frac{\sin^8\theta}{4}+\dots,</math> (22)}}

which may also be written in the form

{{block center|<math>(\text{arc}\sin x)^2=x^2+\frac{2}{3}\,\frac{x^4}{2}+\frac{2.4}{3\cdot5}\,\frac{x^6}{3}+\frac{2.4.6}{3\cdot5\cdot7}\,\frac{x^8}{4}+\dots</math>}}

when <math>x</math> is between <math>\pm1</math>. Differentiating this equation with regard

