which is Gregory's series.

''Series for Calculation of <math>\pi</math>.''—Various series derived from (24) have been employed to calculate the value of <math>\pi</math>. At the end of the 17th century <math>\pi</math> was calculated to 72 places of decimals by Abraham Sharp, by means of the series obtained by putting <math>\text{arc tan}\ y = \pi/6</math>, <math>y = 1/\sqrt{3}</math> in (24). The calculation is to be found in Sherwin's ''Mathematical Tables'' (1742). About the same time J. Machin employed the series obtained from the equation <math>4\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{5} - \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{239} = \tfrac{1}{4}\pi</math> to calculate <math>\pi</math> to 100 decimal places. Long afterwards Euler employed the series obtained from <math>\tfrac{1}{4}\pi = \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{2} + \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{3}</math>, which, however, gives less rapidly converging series (Introd., ''Anal. infin.'' vol. i.). T. F. de Lagny employed the formula <math>\text{arc tan}\ 1/\sqrt{3} = \pi/6</math> to calculate <math>\pi</math> to 127 places; the result was communicated to the Paris Academy in 1719. G. Vega calculated <math>\pi</math> to 140 decimal places by means of the series obtained from the equation <math>\tfrac{1}{4}\pi = 5\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{7} + 2\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{3}{79}</math>. The formula <math>\tfrac{1}{4}\pi = \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{2} + \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{5} + \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{8}</math> was used by J. M. Z. Dase to calculate <math>\pi</math> to 200 decimal places. W. Rutherford used the equation <math>\pi = 4\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{5} - \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{70} + \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{99}</math>.

If in (23) we put <math>y = \tfrac{1}{3}</math> and <math>\tfrac{1}{7}</math>, we have
{{block center|<math>\pi = 8\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{3} + 4\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{7} = 2{\cdot}4\left\{1 + \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{10} + \frac{2.4}{3.5}\,\frac{1}{10^2} + \dots\right\}</math>}}
{{block center|<math>+ {\cdot}56\left\{1 + \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{2}{100} + \frac{2.4}{3.5}\left(\frac{2}{100}\right)^2 + \dots\right\}</math>,}}
a rapidly convergent series for <math>\pi</math> which was first given by Hutton in ''Phil. Trans.'' for 1776, and afterwards by Euler in ''Nova acta'' for 1793. Euler gives an equation deduced in the same manner from the identity <math>\pi = 20\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{1}{7} + 8\ \text{arc tan}\ \tfrac{3}{79}</math>. The calculation of <math>\pi</math> has been carried out to 707 places of decimals; see ''Proc. Roy. Soc.'' vols. xxi. and xxii.; also {{sc|Circle}}.
